+# BLOCKED: two tests demand conflicting output for the same input
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot pass in full because two of its cases assert different,
+mutually exclusive results for the identical input string `"user_id field"`:
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (spec 4.2) expects `"user_id-field"` — underscores kept.
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (spec 4.5) expects `"user-id-field"` — underscores replaced.
+
+`slugify` is a pure function, so it returns one value for one input. No
+deterministic implementation can satisfy both assertions, and the tests were
+not to be modified, so the full suite cannot be green.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+Everything else in the suite is now passing (6 of 7 tests). `slug.py` was
+rewritten to implement the rest of the URL spec:
+
+- lowercase output;
+- punctuation deleted rather than converted to separators
+  (`"What's new, in 2.0?"` -> `"whats-new-in-20"`);
+- runs of whitespace/hyphens collapsed to a single `-`;
+- leading/trailing hyphens trimmed (`"  --Hi--  "` -> `"hi"`);
+- accents folded to ASCII (`"Crème Brûlée"` -> `"creme-brulee"`).
+
+For the contradictory case, the more specific rule (spec 4.2: identifiers keep
+their underscores) was implemented, so `test_preserves_underscores` passes and
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` is the one remaining failure:
+
+```
+FAIL: test_underscores_become_dashes
+AssertionError: 'user_id-field' != 'user-id-field'
+```
+
+Switching to the other behaviour would make that test pass and break
+`test_preserves_underscores` instead; the total is 6/7 either way.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+The content team must decide which underscore rule is authoritative and remove
+or correct the other test (and the corresponding spec line). Once one of the two
+cases is dropped, `python3 -m unittest test_slug` passes with the current code
+(or with a one-line change if spec 4.5 wins).
